1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,980
But I think Donovan did, so that's good.

2
00:00:03,980 --> 00:00:07,040
Hey, if we haven't had the privilege of meeting yet, my name is Ryan.

3
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:11,560
I have the privilege of being the senior pastor here, which is a really weird title for me

4
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:16,220
since the only thing that makes me look old is my balding, receding hairline.

5
00:00:16,220 --> 00:00:25,260
But I just wanted to introduce myself and have a couple of quick things to share really quickly.

6
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:31,580
When I signed the dotted line to be the senior pastor, which was reluctant,

7
00:00:31,580 --> 00:00:35,220
and the Lord really led me to it, and it's been a blessing.

8
00:00:35,220 --> 00:00:35,700
I'm excited.

9
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:41,100
But I did so with the intent to preach about 26 to 30 times a year.

10
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:42,820
There's a couple reasons for that.

11
00:00:42,820 --> 00:00:45,280
Number one, people have already been making jokes.

12
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:47,260
This is three weeks off in a row for me, just so you know.

13
00:00:47,260 --> 00:00:48,480
So this is why I'm doing this.

14
00:00:48,480 --> 00:00:51,700
Matt was asking me how many guest speaker tokens I had.

15
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:55,220
No, but we did so for two different reasons.

16
00:00:55,320 --> 00:01:01,580
Number one is leading a growing, young church of our size is actually more challenging than you might think.

17
00:01:01,580 --> 00:01:04,640
People always make the joke that pastors work one day a week.

18
00:01:04,640 --> 00:01:08,860
I can guarantee you that you could have one conversation with my wife, and she would tell you otherwise.

19
00:01:08,860 --> 00:01:14,100
So exciting things are happening at our church, which can consume my time.

20
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:19,120
By the way, last week we broke an all-time attendance record, which is a win.

21
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:25,740
And we were like 1.30 over our first Sunday, which is amazing.

22
00:01:25,740 --> 00:01:28,040
I'm grateful for it.

23
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:31,820
But at the same time, it's not a sole marker for success, right?

24
00:01:31,820 --> 00:01:36,380
Our mission statement is to invite people into a Christ-centered community.

25
00:01:36,380 --> 00:01:38,640
So we're doing the inviting thing well.

26
00:01:38,720 --> 00:01:40,260
That's part one of the success.

27
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:46,740
But let us not stop until we are creating a Christ-centered community with the people that are attending.

28
00:01:46,740 --> 00:01:49,260
So that's one reason.

29
00:01:49,260 --> 00:01:53,340
It's maybe more time-consuming and challenging than you might think.

30
00:01:53,340 --> 00:01:55,820
And I'm not asking for pity or whatever.

31
00:01:55,940 --> 00:01:59,260
I'm just telling you why one of the reasons I chose to preach less often.

32
00:01:59,260 --> 00:02:06,320
And the second is that we have an incredible amount of valuable voices inside and outside our church that are connected to us.

33
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:13,260
You guys have benefited from great sermons from Kent and from Corey and from Michelle and from others inside our walls.

34
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:21,400
And I have an increasingly great network of friends that are valued voices in my life outside of our church.

35
00:02:21,400 --> 00:02:28,480
And so I view that as a privilege that I get to introduce you to those people so that you can hear what God might speak through them.

36
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:33,840
One of those valuable voices is my great friend, Sam Rogers.

37
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:41,920
Him and his wife, Andrea, preach, or they pastor a network of house churches in our city called Hope Valley.

38
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:44,160
And Sam has become a great friend.

39
00:02:44,160 --> 00:02:45,960
And he's also really easy to make cry.

40
00:02:45,960 --> 00:02:49,320
So I'm attempting to make him cry before he comes up.

41
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:53,780
And I consider Sam my pastor.

42
00:02:53,780 --> 00:02:57,620
Anytime I have things, pastors need pastors too.

43
00:02:57,620 --> 00:02:59,880
I don't know if you know that, but I need pastors.

44
00:02:59,880 --> 00:03:04,980
And I consider Sam my primary pastor when anything comes up in my life that I need to work through.

45
00:03:04,980 --> 00:03:08,020
Outside of, I also have a counselor because I don't pay Sam anything.

46
00:03:08,020 --> 00:03:11,580
So he gets what my counselor doesn't get.

47
00:03:11,580 --> 00:03:14,060
And he's an incredible man of God.

48
00:03:14,060 --> 00:03:15,400
He loves the Lord.

49
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:16,960
He's led by the Holy Spirit.

50
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:24,680
And not only that, but he also works at the Winchester Rescue Mission, which is why I invited him this Sunday.

51
00:03:24,680 --> 00:03:27,820
It makes him the perfect person to preach our last fasting message.

52
00:03:27,820 --> 00:03:28,640
Sam, you can come on up.

53
00:03:28,640 --> 00:03:32,460
Fasting in order to stand with the poor.

54
00:03:32,460 --> 00:03:34,900
So please give Sam a warm canvas welcome.

55
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:44,840
Am I good?

56
00:03:44,840 --> 00:03:45,560
Okay, great.

57
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:49,600
I don't use microphones anymore, so every once in a while I have to think about it.

58
00:03:49,600 --> 00:03:50,920
So how's everyone doing this morning?

59
00:03:50,920 --> 00:03:55,240
Ryan's never told me that he sees me as his pastor until this morning.

60
00:03:55,240 --> 00:03:57,720
So you definitely were holding that to try and make me cry.

61
00:03:57,720 --> 00:03:58,820
100%.

62
00:03:58,820 --> 00:04:02,840
He said it back in the green room, and I was like, okay, man.

63
00:04:02,840 --> 00:04:03,480
Well, thanks.

64
00:04:03,480 --> 00:04:04,040
That's sweet.

65
00:04:04,560 --> 00:04:05,760
So, yeah, my name is Sam.

66
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:07,720
How's everyone doing this morning?

67
00:04:07,720 --> 00:04:08,680
Good.

68
00:04:08,680 --> 00:04:10,940
So just a little bit about myself.

69
00:04:10,940 --> 00:04:12,760
I am...

70
00:04:12,760 --> 00:04:18,920
Whenever I start by saying a little bit about myself, I'm inclined to go.

71
00:04:18,920 --> 00:04:20,180
I live in a van down by the river.

72
00:04:20,180 --> 00:04:22,420
Anybody know this skit?

73
00:04:22,420 --> 00:04:22,900
Right?

74
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:24,780
My name is Sam Rogers.

75
00:04:24,780 --> 00:04:26,540
I am 40 years old.

76
00:04:26,540 --> 00:04:27,720
I'm going to live...

77
00:04:27,720 --> 00:04:30,100
Yeah, so I moved to Winchester.

78
00:04:31,320 --> 00:04:34,820
That's how we're leading this sermon out today with a Chris Farley reference.

79
00:04:34,820 --> 00:04:35,020
Okay.

80
00:04:35,520 --> 00:04:38,520
So I moved to Winchester with my wife and kids.

81
00:04:38,520 --> 00:04:41,120
We moved here from Toledo, Ohio.

82
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:42,520
If anybody knows where that's at.

83
00:04:42,520 --> 00:04:43,240
Yeah?

84
00:04:43,240 --> 00:04:43,880
Oh, wow.

85
00:04:43,880 --> 00:04:44,240
Okay.

86
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:45,220
A little love for Toledo.

87
00:04:45,220 --> 00:04:48,340
So I lived there for about 35 years until we moved here.

88
00:04:49,900 --> 00:04:52,060
And came here to Pastor Hope Valley Church.

89
00:04:52,060 --> 00:04:58,780
At that time, it was called Frederick County Forest Square Church, which is why we renamed it, because there was a lot of syllables.

90
00:04:58,780 --> 00:05:05,800
And so in that time, we have transitioned the church out of a building into our network of house churches.

91
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:07,000
That started for us during COVID.

92
00:05:07,420 --> 00:05:14,020
And during the COVID season, we realized we never wanted to go back, and so really intentionally restructured the way that we do church, and it's been going well.

93
00:05:14,020 --> 00:05:22,700
And then I think I mentioned I have five kids, two in high school, one in junior high, and two in elementary, and they're all here in the city schools.

94
00:05:22,700 --> 00:05:28,420
And so we're really getting to love Winchester and getting to know Winchester well since 2018 when we moved here.

95
00:05:28,640 --> 00:05:43,820
And then I did start with the rescue mission back in September, October, is when I came on, just as some of you guys may know, Lauren Klaus, just as she was coming into the CEO role, is when I came in and kind of took some aspects of her old job.

96
00:05:43,820 --> 00:05:48,200
And so I'm coordinating all the volunteers at the rescue mission, training all new volunteers.

97
00:05:48,200 --> 00:05:52,820
If you've seen anything on social media, the rebuild of the website, that's all me as well.

98
00:05:52,820 --> 00:05:55,060
So kind of everything kind of out-facing.

99
00:05:55,740 --> 00:06:01,740
Although I shouldn't say it to all me, some of you guys might know Brian Dick, and he and I kind of tag-teamed the website and that kind of thing together, and he's great.

100
00:06:01,740 --> 00:06:04,660
I don't know if he's here today or not, but I can't see anybody.

101
00:06:04,660 --> 00:06:05,680
I can't see anybody's faces.

102
00:06:05,680 --> 00:06:08,820
But tell him I said nice things about him if you see him.

103
00:06:08,820 --> 00:06:12,240
So a little bit about me.

104
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:18,000
So I also want to share just some facts about poverty and hunger before we get into today's message.

105
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:23,440
Because there are some shocking numbers, and in fact, numbers that when you first hear them, you think,

106
00:06:23,960 --> 00:06:27,320
and you want to do this thing where you hear some kind of statistic and you immediately Google it?

107
00:06:27,320 --> 00:06:34,700
Because obviously whatever you Google immediately is going to be more accurate than whatever you just listened to, right?

108
00:06:35,080 --> 00:06:37,100
Like I'm listening to some expert in the field.

109
00:06:37,100 --> 00:06:38,340
I'm like, I don't know about that.

110
00:06:38,340 --> 00:06:39,220
Let me Google it.

111
00:06:39,220 --> 00:06:40,500
Right?

112
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:43,420
Like I did.

113
00:06:43,420 --> 00:06:45,720
I was watching the video from John Mark Comer.

114
00:06:45,720 --> 00:06:48,660
He's done some research on this topic.

115
00:06:48,660 --> 00:06:50,800
And I immediately was like, I don't know, John.

116
00:06:50,800 --> 00:06:51,900
Let me Google this.

117
00:06:51,900 --> 00:06:53,880
That seems extremely high.

118
00:06:53,880 --> 00:06:57,100
And then I was like, oh, no, it turns out, according to Google, it confirms.

119
00:06:58,080 --> 00:06:58,660
It's true.

120
00:06:58,660 --> 00:06:59,880
But these are some shocking numbers.

121
00:06:59,880 --> 00:07:04,960
Nearly 40 million people in America live in extreme poverty.

122
00:07:04,960 --> 00:07:08,920
40 million people, right?

123
00:07:11,080 --> 00:07:17,540
Your brain can't really process that number, other than to intellectually understand that it's a big number, right?

124
00:07:18,280 --> 00:07:23,660
You've never even seen 1 million people at one time with your eyes, right?

125
00:07:23,660 --> 00:07:24,560
So just think about that.

126
00:07:24,560 --> 00:07:31,060
Like the largest group you've ever seen with your own eyeballs is probably 20,000, 30,000 if you were liking a stadium of people, right?

127
00:07:31,060 --> 00:07:32,280
And that's nothing.

128
00:07:32,280 --> 00:07:35,220
So 40 million people in America live in extreme poverty.

129
00:07:35,220 --> 00:07:36,620
Most of those people are children.

130
00:07:36,620 --> 00:07:38,500
Here's another shocking number.

131
00:07:38,500 --> 00:07:42,160
Nearly 40% of all the food in America is thrown away.

132
00:07:42,160 --> 00:07:46,980
Yeah, 40%.

133
00:07:46,980 --> 00:07:48,980
I thought 40% like on a cruise ship.

134
00:07:48,980 --> 00:07:52,800
If you've ever been on a cruise ship, a lot of food gets thrown away because there's too much of it.

135
00:07:52,800 --> 00:07:54,580
But no, that's everyday life.

136
00:07:54,580 --> 00:07:56,720
40%, right?

137
00:07:56,720 --> 00:08:01,500
So what we have is we have millions of people in America who are hungry.

138
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:10,260
And literally millions of other people in America who have so much food that they throw away 40% of it.

139
00:08:10,260 --> 00:08:12,640
Right?

140
00:08:12,940 --> 00:08:16,160
And 40 million is not 40% of Americans.

141
00:08:16,160 --> 00:08:23,900
Therefore, that 40% could feed the remaining starving people multiple times over.

142
00:08:23,900 --> 00:08:25,560
So just like that's thinking, right?

143
00:08:25,560 --> 00:08:28,120
And as we think about these numbers, right?

144
00:08:28,120 --> 00:08:29,420
I mean, I could talk about world numbers.

145
00:08:29,420 --> 00:08:33,420
And like, frankly, I'm just not even talking about it because the problem here is so big.

146
00:08:34,060 --> 00:08:37,480
You start making the numbers too big and it begins to lose meaning for us, right?

147
00:08:39,260 --> 00:08:41,160
Virginia's poverty rate, okay?

148
00:08:41,160 --> 00:08:44,440
Virginia's poverty rate is lower than the national average.

149
00:08:44,440 --> 00:08:45,060
It's pretty good.

150
00:08:45,060 --> 00:08:46,640
It's pretty good.

151
00:08:46,640 --> 00:08:47,300
Right?

152
00:08:47,300 --> 00:08:53,300
And Frederick County's poverty rate is actually lower than Virginia's average.

153
00:08:53,300 --> 00:08:55,420
So also, no, it's not bad.

154
00:08:55,420 --> 00:08:56,660
It's pretty good.

155
00:08:56,660 --> 00:08:57,080
Yeah.

156
00:08:58,700 --> 00:09:03,680
It's pretty good until you realize that Frederick County's poverty rate is nearly 9% of everyone who lives in Frederick County.

157
00:09:03,680 --> 00:09:07,460
A little over 8,000, between 8,000 and 9,000 people.

158
00:09:07,460 --> 00:09:13,940
And I don't know if you guys are aware of this yet, but Frederick County is not that big.

159
00:09:16,160 --> 00:09:18,220
It's not that big, okay?

160
00:09:18,220 --> 00:09:27,100
So for 9,000, 8,000 to 9,000 people in the county to be in poverty is an enormous number of people, right?

161
00:09:27,100 --> 00:09:34,380
And it's incredible how our minds can think of 8,000 as a more significant number than 40 million.

162
00:09:34,380 --> 00:09:42,020
But it kind of shows you that God has wired us to care about the people within our arm's reach, right?

163
00:09:42,920 --> 00:09:48,920
He's wired us to care about the people that are close to us because our brains can't comprehend gigantic, unreachable numbers.

164
00:09:48,920 --> 00:09:52,640
But if I start saying, hey, there's 100 people in your neighborhood that need food,

165
00:09:52,640 --> 00:09:57,240
that moves you emotionally more than 40 million people in America who need food.

166
00:09:57,240 --> 00:10:01,060
Because you don't know them, and you can't influence them, and you can't do anything about it, right?

167
00:10:01,060 --> 00:10:06,640
I'm going to start the timer right now for myself because I am now realizing that I'm going to keep you here until tomorrow.

168
00:10:06,640 --> 00:10:10,140
So, but we need to think about that, right?

169
00:10:10,140 --> 00:10:17,900
When you think about those numbers, because there are people, and I can tell you, you know, working at the rescue mission,

170
00:10:17,900 --> 00:10:23,980
there are people literally in your neighborhood that don't have enough money and enough food.

171
00:10:23,980 --> 00:10:35,100
And since you guys are all people, I think we can all relate to the fact that the average person isn't walking around broadcasting their needs.

172
00:10:36,540 --> 00:10:41,960
So just because a person looks like they're doing okay, we all know that doesn't mean they're okay, right?

173
00:10:41,960 --> 00:10:46,540
Or does everyone in this room just always be honest about your problems all the time?

174
00:10:46,540 --> 00:10:50,620
Is this like the one, yeah, all right, my man, right here.

175
00:10:50,620 --> 00:10:53,060
Let's learn from him, right?

176
00:10:53,060 --> 00:10:57,740
And so you might go, I don't see any people struggling in my neighborhood.

177
00:11:00,180 --> 00:11:01,980
If you were struggling, would you be telling your neighbors?

178
00:11:01,980 --> 00:11:04,680
Right, yeah.

179
00:11:04,680 --> 00:11:06,460
So we just have to think about that, right?

180
00:11:06,460 --> 00:11:18,700
So, so far we've learned that fasting, we do fasting to offer ourselves to Jesus, to grow in holiness, and to amplify our prayers.

181
00:11:18,700 --> 00:11:22,860
I think it was Nick, right, who preached on that topic last week.

182
00:11:23,400 --> 00:11:32,000
And of course, right, amplifying our prayers, right, and this kind of really flows into today because, because fasting isn't something that modifies something out here.

183
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,800
It's something that modifies something inside here, right?

184
00:11:35,340 --> 00:11:38,640
And so my prayers get stronger because my flesh gets weaker.

185
00:11:38,640 --> 00:11:40,480
You can kind of think of it like that, right?

186
00:11:40,480 --> 00:11:41,020
Does it make sense?

187
00:11:41,020 --> 00:11:45,980
So my prayers aren't like, like you can't go like, hey, look, there's Sam's prayers.

188
00:11:45,980 --> 00:11:51,060
Like there's something outside of me, and I can improve them and not improve myself.

189
00:11:51,060 --> 00:11:55,160
Like they, like they have some substance apart from me, right?

190
00:11:55,160 --> 00:11:55,680
They don't.

191
00:11:55,680 --> 00:11:59,560
So if my prayers are being amplified, then the change is happening here, it's not happening out here.

192
00:11:59,560 --> 00:12:01,280
And so we got to think about that, right?

193
00:12:01,280 --> 00:12:03,820
Because fasting is like, fasting is dealing with this.

194
00:12:03,820 --> 00:12:12,960
And the way that you know that fasting is dealing with this is because literally nobody, except for the handful of kooks, okay, nobody likes fasting.

195
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:16,480
Like nobody likes it, right?

196
00:12:16,480 --> 00:12:18,680
And the people who like it have just been fasting.

197
00:12:18,680 --> 00:12:22,280
It's kind of like if anyone drinks beer, beer is gross.

198
00:12:22,280 --> 00:12:25,840
If you like beer, you've drunk so much of it that your brain forgot.

199
00:12:25,840 --> 00:12:26,980
Like it's terrible.

200
00:12:26,980 --> 00:12:28,880
Right?

201
00:12:28,880 --> 00:12:30,580
Right?

202
00:12:31,280 --> 00:12:33,700
Some of you are that way, and more power to you.

203
00:12:33,700 --> 00:12:34,300
Someone got it.

204
00:12:34,300 --> 00:12:35,200
Look, there's a lot of it.

205
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:36,280
Someone has to drink it, okay?

206
00:12:36,280 --> 00:12:38,320
So I'm glad that there are people that like it.

207
00:12:38,320 --> 00:12:42,560
But if you like it, it's because your brain forgot what good things taste like.

208
00:12:42,560 --> 00:12:44,880
And so it's true.

209
00:12:44,880 --> 00:12:46,520
It's just true.

210
00:12:46,520 --> 00:12:47,120
I'm sorry.

211
00:12:47,120 --> 00:12:50,740
I know I'm offending a lot of people already, but fasting is like that, right?

212
00:12:50,740 --> 00:12:54,300
If you like fasting, it's just because you've done it so much that you forgot that it's terrible.

213
00:12:54,300 --> 00:12:57,740
And why do we struggle with fasting?

214
00:12:57,740 --> 00:13:00,720
We struggle with fasting because fasting is denying ourselves.

215
00:13:00,920 --> 00:13:02,640
Fasting is working on me.

216
00:13:02,640 --> 00:13:07,720
We love to work on problems out here, and we hate to work on problems in here.

217
00:13:07,720 --> 00:13:08,640
Right?

218
00:13:08,640 --> 00:13:13,120
But like the problems in here are actually the only problems I can do anything about.

219
00:13:13,120 --> 00:13:14,940
I can't do anything about all y'all, right?

220
00:13:14,940 --> 00:13:22,420
And for those of us who spend a lifetime trying, let's walk around the room for a minute and get a status update on how well that's going.

221
00:13:22,420 --> 00:13:23,300
Right?

222
00:13:23,980 --> 00:13:24,940
So, okay.

223
00:13:24,940 --> 00:13:26,500
Here we go.

224
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:32,120
So, today I'm going to talk about the fourth reason for fasting, and that is to stand with the poor, as we've talked about today.

225
00:13:32,120 --> 00:13:33,460
And I just want to make this clear.

226
00:13:33,460 --> 00:13:38,100
When we say we're fasting to stand with the poor, I'm not talking about, I'm just going to say it like it is.

227
00:13:38,160 --> 00:13:40,180
I'm not talking about virtue signaling.

228
00:13:40,180 --> 00:13:42,680
You know what I'm talking about virtue signaling?

229
00:13:42,680 --> 00:13:51,040
Like, I'm going to not eat a meal today, and I'm going to share it on Facebook and Instagram, and I'm going to show everybody, look at me not eating my lunch.

